<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comparison of performance and data usage of DPO-augmented vs. <span id="S5.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Full</span> models at <math id="S5.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1 Full</span> denotes the macro F1 scores of <span id="S5.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Full</span> models that use the entire available training data. <span id="S5.T2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1%</span> is the percentage of the F1 score attained by the corresponding DPO-augmented model over <span id="S5.T2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1 Full</span>. <span id="S5.T2.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Data%</span> is the percentage of size of the training data using <math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math> shot over the full training samples. ModernBERT’s scores are presented for references.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Remarks</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our empirical results demonstrates that our DPO augmentation enables LLMs to achieve considerable performance with low resource. From <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2.1 Results for 𝐾&lt;=128 ‣ 5.2 Classification Results ‣ 5 Stage 1: Individual Model Training ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>, DPO-augmented models attain high macro F1 scores at <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=64</annotation></semantics></math>, a very small amount of data. <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2.2 Results for 𝐾=256 ‣ 5.2 Classification Results ‣ 5 Stage 1: Individual Model Training ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 2</span></a> further showcases that DPO-augmented models can achieve at least 86% of F1 performance (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span>) or even exceed that of their <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Full</span> counterpart (<span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latent Hate</span>) while using only fraction of the available training data at <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math>. For practitioners, these insights suggest a resource-conservative strategy: start with low K-shot samples (64) to obtain benchmark results, then iteratively add more training samples until performance plateaus or resource is exhausted.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Stage 2: Cross-model Refinement</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we focus on investigating methods to identify and efficiently improve the LLMs’ quality of explanations with respect to certain desired directions. We first enlist human annotators to evaluate the explanations by <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> and <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span>, then experiment with further training one model with the other’s outputs to simultaneously improve performance and quality of explanations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Human Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to resource constraints, we only perform this evaluation pipeline for <span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span>. We opt to collect outputs from the SFT+DPO models at <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve the rest of the training data for further refinement experiments. To make a fair comparison on the outputs to agree on the predicted label, we use the prompt in <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix C Obtaining Explanations for In-domain Data ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 7</span></a> to obtain explanations conditioned on the correct gold label. This technique allows us to obtain 342 samples with consistent labels, or 89% of the possible <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128*3=384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128*3=384</annotation></semantics></math> possible samples. The other 42 samples contain discrepant predicted labels, where the LLMs revert to behaviors ingrained by previous training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To simulate real-life deployment, we solicit 14 crowdworkers from diverse demographic backgrounds on the platform Amazon Mechanical Turks (MTurks) to annotate their preference on the explanations offered by the LLMs in this study . Annotators are encouraged to evaluate the pairs of explanation for each post based on the criteria of <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Clarity, Reasoning</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Alignment</span> as illustrated with the template in <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix A Amazon Turks Mechanical Annotation ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 6</span></a>. To avoid position bias, we present the pairs of explanations in randomized orders. All annotators are compensated fairly.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S6.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Label</span></th>
<th id="S6.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T5</span></th>
<th id="S6.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama</span></th>
<th id="S6.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Row Total</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Normal</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">25 (25.5%)</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">73 (74.5%)</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">98</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Offensive</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">63 (49.6%)</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">64 (50.4%)</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">127</td></tr>
