<td id="S6.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Hate</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">54 (46.2%)</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">63 (53.8%)</td>
<td id="S6.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">117</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Results of preference for explanations by MTurk annotators based on majority voting for 342 <span id="S6.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span> samples.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Annotation Results</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.1 Human Evaluation ‣ 6 Stage 2: Cross-model Refinement ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 3</span></a> shows the annotation results, aggregated per sample by majority voting. Across the 3 labels in <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span>, annotators show no significant preference for explanations by either model on <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Offensive</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hate</span>. On the other hand, <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span>’s explanations for <span id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Normal</span> posts are overwhelmingly preferred.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Cross-model Stylistic Refinement</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span> produces outputs preferred by human annotators, a natural choice is to select this model for ultimate deployment. We are nevertheless interested in examining the potential to steer one model’s explanation style to align with another model’s while maintaining existing classification performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Using the prompting technique described in section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Human Evaluation ‣ 6 Stage 2: Cross-model Refinement ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>, we use the SFT+DPO <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> variants of both <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> LLMs to generate explanations conditioned on the gold labels of the held-out 128-shot portion of the training data, which neither of these models has seen. We then perform cross-model SFT and DPO finetune using this dataset. More specifically, we further finetune the existing SFT+DPO <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> variant using explanations produced by its <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span> counterpart, and vice versa. This step consists of both separate SFT and then DPO self-augmentation as in section <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 K-shot In-domain Finetuning ‣ 5 Stage 1: Individual Model Training ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each test domain, we additionally train a style classifier based on the BERT architecture using explanations generated individually by <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span> of the SFT+DPO variant at <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Devlin et al. (2019)</a></cite>. A classification head is added to BERT, which leverages the <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[CLS]</span> token from each explanation’s embedding to predict whether it was produced by <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> or <span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLaMA</span>. Technical details are reported in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Preference Optimziation ‣ Appendix E Technical Details ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.1 </span>Cross-model Refinement Results</h4>

<figure id="S6.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15174v1/xtrain_graphic_HateXplain.svg" id="S6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/225;" width="452" height="225"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15174v1/xtrain_graphic_Latent_Hate.svg" id="S6.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/225;" width="452" height="225"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15174v1/xtrain_graphic_Implicit_Hate.svg" id="S6.F4.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/225;" width="452" height="225"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Macro-F1 scores on test portion of the 3 test sets for <span id="S6.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> and <span id="S6.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span> cross-model refinement regimen. In each figure: the first and last bars are scores reported with only <span id="S6.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> model at <math id="S6.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math> (directly from <a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 5.2.1 Results for 𝐾&lt;=128 ‣ 5.2 Classification Results ‣ 5 Stage 1: Individual Model Training ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>); the middle bars are results after further finetuned using data from the complementary <math id="S6.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> shots of counterpart model. Split-color bars: bottom color indicates the original model; top color indicates the counterpart model for additional cross-training. </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.2.1 Cross-model Refinement Results ‣ 6.2 Cross-model Stylistic Refinement ‣ 6 Stage 2: Cross-model Refinement ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>shows the macro-F1 scores of both the original single-model SFT+DPO and cross-model finetuned variants on the test datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-model training enhances T5 while impairing Llama.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Applying SFT training using explanations from <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span> improves performance over the baseline <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> SFT+DPO on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span> (F1 scores 0.62 from 0.60) and <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latent Hate</span> (0.64 from 0.62), with a slight reduction on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit Hate</span> (0.54 from 0.56). On the other hand, applying SFT+DPO augmentation using <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span>’s explanations from the complementary K=128 shot data allows T5 to <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">exceed</span> single-model performance on the full K=256 set across the board. The new maximum macro-F1 scores for <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> are 0.66 on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span> (up from 0.62 of single-model), 0.66 on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latent Hate</span> (up from 0.65), and 0.61 on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit Hate</span> (up from 0.59). Notably, these scores are superior to even <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span>’s single-model performance at <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math> on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit Hate</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, SFT cross-training with <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span>’s output reduces <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLama</span>’s F1 scores to 0.58 from 0.63 on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span> while improving to 0.66 from 0.62 on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latent Hate</span> and plateauing at 0.58 on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit Hate</span>. Additional SFT+DPO cross-model augmentation slightly boosts performance on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span> and <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Implicit Hate</span> while slightly impairing on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Latent Hate</span>. None of the cross-model <span id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span> variants matches the single-models’ F1 scores at <math id="S6.SS2.SSS1.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15174v1/xmodel_bert_hatexplain.svg" id="S6.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/139;" width="476" height="139"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>HateXplain</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15174v1/xmodel_bert_latent_hate.svg" id="S6.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/139;" width="476" height="139"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Latent Hate</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S6.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15174v1/xmodel_bert_implicit_hate.svg" id="S6.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/176;" width="476" height="176"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>Implicit Hate</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Percentage distribution of <span id="S6.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span>- and <span id="S6.F5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span>-style explanations on test sets by BERT style classifiers, pre (<span id="S6.F5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Base</span>) and post cross-model (<span id="S6.F5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Xmod</span>) refined. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.2.2 </span>Stylistic Analysis</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Cross-model training enhances T5 while impairing Llama. ‣ 6.2.1 Cross-model Refinement Results ‣ 6.2 Cross-model Stylistic Refinement ‣ 6 Stage 2: Cross-model Refinement ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 5</span></a>, most explanations from the individually trained models exhibit their corresponding style, while cross-model SFT fine-tuning is sufficient to substantially align the outputs with the alternative’s style. With the exception of <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> on <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HateXplain</span>, the application of self-augmenting DPO using cross-model data generally amplifies the stylistic presence of the base model to a small extent, suggesting that this mechanism induces a mild converging effect on the reasoning style.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-model explanations improve weak categories.</h5>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze how cross-model refinement enhances classification by comparing <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Precision</span>, <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall</span>, and <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">F1-score</span> breakdowns of the base models and their cross-trained counterparts at <math id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=256</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#A4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Appendix D Cross-model Analysis ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 8</span></a>. Models often improve in categories where they initially struggled but their counterpart performed well, suggesting they can adopt effective reasoning patterns. However, performance may decline if the counterpart model is weak. This tendency, along with the <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stylistic transference</span> shown in <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Cross-model training enhances T5 while impairing Llama. ‣ 6.2.1 Cross-model Refinement Results ‣ 6.2 Cross-model Stylistic Refinement ‣ 6 Stage 2: Cross-model Refinement ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 5</span></a>, aligns with findings on the benefits of <span id="S6.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning diversity</span> by incorporating outputs from other models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al. ()</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chenglin et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion and Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Informed by empirical insights , we offer the following recommendations for practitioners.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Cross-model refinement can further improve performance</h5>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span>’s superior F1-scores compared to individually trained models (<a href="#S6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 6.2.1 Cross-model Refinement Results ‣ 6.2 Cross-model Stylistic Refinement ‣ 6 Stage 2: Cross-model Refinement ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>) suggest added value in leveraging competing models. Practitioners can train multiple LLMs using the SFT+DPO framework across data thresholds, then select a high-quality checkpoint (e.g., <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=128</annotation></semantics></math> from Section <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS1" title="5.2.1 Results for 𝐾&lt;=128 ‣ 5.2 Classification Results ‣ 5 Stage 1: Individual Model Training ‣ SMARTER: A Data-efficient Framework to Improve Toxicity Detection with Explanation via Self-augmenting Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2.1</span></a>) for further finetuning with outputs from other models. Though additional performance gain may not be guaranteed, cross-model refinement is still a venue worthy of exploration particularly when resources are limited.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Architectural diversity may offer benefits.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The differing post-training behaviors of <span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> and <span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span>—despite being trained on the same seed explanations—may stem from their distinct architectures (encoder-decoder vs. encoder-only). We hypothesize, based on prior work, that the stronger model (<span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama</span>) enhances reasoning diversity that <span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">T5</span> can leverage. While further study is needed for a definitive explanation, our findings suggest practitioners may benefit from using architecturally diverse models within this framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human oversight is still essential.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the initial annotation, human supervision is necessary to monitor the stylistic quality of explanations, especially in iterative-development settings where unexpected patterns may occur after multiple rounds of cross-model training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We hope that SMARTER will enable practitioners to effectively and efficiently deploy explanation-based content moderation pipelines.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We discuss several limitations in our research, along with their implications for future explorations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Generalization beyond English</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work focuses exclusively on English corpora. In reality, social media exists for every language. As both English-based LLMs and research literature often attract much more attention, we hope this framework will be adapted for other languages.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model selection</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While we perform extensive experiments, our selection of 2 models could be expanded. At the cost of more training resource, training more models with different architectures or generation offer extra options for performance comparison for mix-and-match cross-training. We encourage other researchers to experiment with more LLMs using our framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limited scope of human validation</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to budget constraints, we are only able to human validation for 1 dataset. In practice, it is recommended to solicit a larger pool of annotators to ensure appropriate amount of human oversight.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Risk of bias in automated explanation</h5>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our framework aims to improve performance and versatility in content moderation, it does not alleviate the fundamental sensitive nature of this task. Content moderation could be misappropriated to suppress free speech or harm marginalized groups <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dias Oliva (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozyreva et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Other works have noted that LLMs exhibit bias behaviors in certain applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. We urge researchers and practitioners to maintain vigilance when integrating our framework with human supervision to ensure ethic standards <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lai et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Ethical Considerations</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors are not aware of any ethical problems in the development of this work. This research uses publicly available dataset. We also further synonymize the content to the best of our ability to provide extra caution for privacy. We acknowledge that our framework and associated techniques could be abused for harmful purposes.</p>
</div>
</section>
